# Line endings (a Windows checkout must be byte-identical to a Linux one; tests compare bytes). The index holds LF; this file only decides what a
# checkout writes. git ls-files --eol must show no i/crlf, and `git add --renormalize .` must change nothing.
#
# Everything git takes for text is written with LF whatever core.autocrlf says (scripts, JSON, TSV, Markdown, YAML, mjs).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files whose BYTES are read or compared by tests, fixtures and golden files: never converted, never normalised (a byte string may hold CR, NUL or a
# missing final newline, and the expected hashes are over those bytes). tests/ci/gitattributes.test.mjs checks that every tests/golden file is covered.
tests/golden/** -text
tests/enforcement/a132-commands/** -text
tests/e2e/replies/** -text
tests/legacy-hook/** -text
tests/legacy-githooks/** -text
tests/skills/fixtures/** -text
fixtures/** -text

# Binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.pdf binary
